WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.520
Even though Tusken Raiders show up in Star Wars&nbsp;
from the very beginning and reappear throughout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:11.040
they remain mysterious and poorly understood.&nbsp;
Who are they? Where do they come from? What do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.960
they want? And what do fans and casual&nbsp;
viewers alike need to know about them?

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.840
The Tusken Raiders appear first&nbsp;
and most memorably in A New Hope.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:25.120
The Sand People are initially presented as&nbsp;
fearsome savages, dangers to "civilized" people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:30.800
like Luke Skywalker and his family. They roam the&nbsp;
desert, attacking and pillaging the unsuspecting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:36.240
When Luke leaves the safety of his homestead to&nbsp;
search the Jundland Wastes for his escaped droid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:41.280
R2-D2, the Raiders quickly overpower&nbsp;
the naive and unprepared young man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:46.480
knocking him unconscious so they can ransack&nbsp;
his land speeder. Luke is only saved when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:52.800
mad hermit Ben Kenobi appears and scares away the&nbsp;
Raiders by mimicking the call of the Krayt Dragon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.840
a beast they fear. Despite his ability&nbsp;
to scare them away, Ben also warns Luke:

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.120
"The Jundland Wastes are&nbsp;
not to be traveled lightly."

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.240
Of course if Luke had died in this scene,&nbsp;
he never would have blown up the Death Star,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:10.400
fought Darth Vader, become a Jedi&nbsp;
Knight, and helped defeat the Empire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:15.440
Perhaps the trilogy would have centered on Leia&nbsp;
Organa, Luke's Force-sensitive sister, instead.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.400
Star Wars mastermind George Lucas&nbsp;
created the Tusken Raiders and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.960
their desert world to resemble the&nbsp;
Bedouins, tribes of Arab nomads.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:30.480
However, it's likely Lucas' conception of the&nbsp;
Raiders was also influenced by the so-called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:36.400
"savage Indians" of John Ford Westerns, especially&nbsp;
from The Searchers, which Star Wars famously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:41.920
pays homage to. In The Searchers, Comanche&nbsp;
warriors attack the white settlers' homestead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:46.160
killing the family and burning the house&nbsp;
down. While it's the Imperial stormtroopers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:51.120
and not the Tusken Raiders, that exterminate&nbsp;
Luke's family, Lucas designed the scene in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:55.040
direct homage to the scene from The&nbsp;
Searchers. In general, Lucas depicts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.040 --> 00:02:00.800
the Sand People the way that Ford depicts the&nbsp;
Comanche: brutal, bloodthirsty and uncivilized.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:06.320
In terms of the name, "Tusken" sounds quite a&nbsp;
bit like "Tuskegee," as in the Tuskegee Airmen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:11.680
the first Black U.S. Army airmen who trained&nbsp;
at the Tuskegee Army Air Field in Alabama&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:17.200
before flying in combat during World War II.&nbsp;
Lucas has long been interested in their story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:23.360
even making a movie about them with 2012's Red&nbsp;
Tails. Finally, Lucas was influenced by Frank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:28.560
Herbert's sci-fi novel Dune, which features&nbsp;
nomad dwellers on the desert planet Arrakis.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:35.200
Banthas are large, trundling creatures that&nbsp;
resemble elephants with huge, droopy faces.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:40.240
They have curving tusks on the sides of their&nbsp;
heads shaped like cornucopias, or perhaps Princess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:45.840
Leia's hair buns in A New Hope. The Raiders use&nbsp;
them as beasts of burden. Lucas clearly liked the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:51.280
Banthas, as he digitally inserted a lot more of&nbsp;
them in the special edition of Return of the Jedi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:56.400
which features a herd of "wild" Banthas roaming&nbsp;
the Tatooine desert. They are seen as Jabba the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:01.920
Hutt transports Luke, Han Solo, and company&nbsp;
across the Dune Sea to the Pit of Carkoon to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:07.760
be slowly digested over a thousand years by the&nbsp;
mighty Sarlacc. This is an appropriate fate, since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:13.760
Jabba had previously told Han, "You may have been&nbsp;
a good smuggler, but now you're bantha fodder."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:18.240
Digital banthas also appear briefly in The&nbsp;
Phantom Menace and Attack of the Clones.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:23.120
Chapter 9 of The Mandalorian also heavily&nbsp;
features Banthas. Early in the episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:27.760
the Krayt Dragon - a huge worm-like&nbsp;
creature - swallows one whole. Later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:31.440
a Tusken Raider brushes a Bantha's&nbsp;
teeth, and in the climactic scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:36.640
a Bantha is rigged with explosives and used&nbsp;
to bait the Krayt. The dragon swallows the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:41.680
Bantha bomb and the Mandalorian detonates it,&nbsp;
causing both animals to explode in a shower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:47.360
of flame and flesh. In other news, the ASPCA&nbsp;
does not appear to have a Tatooine chapter.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:53.120
While it's true that Banthas provide the Tusken&nbsp;
Raiders with a means of transportation across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:58.160
Tatooine's unforgiving terrain, they&nbsp;
aren't merely beasts of burden. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:03.280
every Tusken has a deep lifelong connection with&nbsp;
their Bantha - the culture's initiation rite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:07.680
which typically takes place during youth,&nbsp;
includes gifting the initiate a Bantha that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:12.800
matches their gender. When Tuskens marry, their&nbsp;
Banthas mate as well - and in the unfortunate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:16.640
circumstance that a Bantha should die&nbsp;
before its rider, tradition calls for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:21.200
its remains to be interred in a graveyard&nbsp;
with sacred meaning to the Tusken people.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:24.640
One of the Star Wars saga's&nbsp;
strongest points is the way in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:28.640
even incidental elements can be fully&nbsp;
fleshed out into stories of their own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:32.320
and the bond between the Banthas and&nbsp;
the Sand People is a perfect example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:36.880
They may have served as little more than a&nbsp;
brief, terrifying plot point in A New Hope,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:41.840
but the hidden depths to their story offer some&nbsp;
of the franchise's most compelling allegories.

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:47.200
The Tuskens have a cameo during the&nbsp;
Episode I podracing sequence and their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:51.760
brief appearance in the movie echoes their&nbsp;
aggressive characterization from A New Hope.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:57.040
In the scene, young Anakin Skywalker is racing&nbsp;
the villainous Sebulba to gain his freedom from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:01.920
slavery when the Tuskens attempt to disrupt the&nbsp;
race. From their perch in the rocky hills above&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:06.880
the desert race track, the Raiders take pot shots&nbsp;
at the racers with their long-barreled rifles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:11.840
and celebrate when they hit Anakin's pod. Whether&nbsp;
the Tuskens are doing this for vicious sport&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:16.400
or whether they feel they are protecting their&nbsp;
territory from interlopers is never specified.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:21.200
Either way, the brief scene sets the stage for&nbsp;
the Tuskens' longer appearance in Episode II,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:25.760
a movie that seems to use their established&nbsp;
villainy to justify their wholesale slaughter.

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:31.680
Tusken Raiders feature prominently in Episode&nbsp;
II: Attack of the Clones, in which they kidnap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:36.960
and brutalize Anakin's mother, Shmi Skywalker.&nbsp;
Anakin sneaks into their camp to retrieve her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:41.040
but he is too late and she dies in&nbsp;
his arms. In a fit of vengeful rage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:44.880
he exterminates every Raider in the&nbsp;
camp, including women and children.

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:49.920
This scene appears to further equate the Sand&nbsp;
People with cinematic Native American violence in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:55.040
Lucas' vision. The kidnapping and slaughter in&nbsp;
Attack of the Clones again echoes The Searchers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:59.280
when the title characters discover&nbsp;
kidnapped white women in Comanche tents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:04.640
similar to the ones in Episode II. Later, John&nbsp;
Wayne's character Ethan gleefully participates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:09.440
in a bloody slaughter of the Comanche, which&nbsp;
includes scalping them. Anakin also appears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:14.240
to relish his butchery, and tells Padme that&nbsp;
he simply slaughtered them like "animals."

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:18.640
Although Lucas means to portray Anakin's&nbsp;
killing as heartless - a significant step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:21.760
toward his embrace of the Dark&nbsp;
Side - the director's sympathies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:25.840
seem to lie at least partially with&nbsp;
Anakin. Anakin may be in the wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:30.000
but the Raiders are nevertheless depicted&nbsp;
as sub-creatures, undeserving of pity.

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:35.520
Like most pop culture properties that have&nbsp;
been around for decades, Star Wars has tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:40.000
to keep up with the times by reflecting&nbsp;
social and cultural changes. In particular,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:44.960
the franchise has attempted to be more diverse&nbsp;
in its race and gender representation, an effort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:50.080
that has resulted in backlash from some fans who&nbsp;
don't care for change. The Mandalorian has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:54.720
part of this effort, both with its casting and&nbsp;
behind the camera. It even attempts a makeover&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.720 --> 00:07:00.000
of the Tusken Raiders by representing them as a&nbsp;
less overtly aggressive and more complex culture.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.040
The more complex portrait of the&nbsp;
Sand People of The Mandalorian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:08.320
debuts in Chapter 5 of Season 1. When another&nbsp;
character implies that the Tuskens are "filth"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:11.120
who bother "the locals," the Mandalorian responds,

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:16.052
"Tuskens think they're the locals.&nbsp;
Everyone else is just trespassing."

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:21.040
He then peacefully attains passage through&nbsp;
their land by negotiating with them in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:26.720
sign language. This is the first notion anywhere&nbsp;
in filmed Star Wars that Raiders have a language,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:30.800
let alone one that allows them to communicate&nbsp;
with other peoples. Language is one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:36.160
most important markers of civilization and this&nbsp;
episode presents it as a feature of the Tuskens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:40.000
who are no longer depicted as the&nbsp;
backwards savages of previous eras.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:46.480
After their cameo in Chapter 5 of The Mandalorian,&nbsp;
the Tuskens return in the action-packed Season 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.480 --> 00:07:51.680
opener in an even more vital role. In this&nbsp;
episode, the Mandalorian enlists their help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:56.400
to kill a Krayt Dragon. This quest returns&nbsp;
full circle to the saga's introduction of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:01.360
Sand People in A New Hope, when Ben Kenobi's&nbsp;
Krayt noises frighten them away from Luke.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:06.000
But this time we see the actual beast, and&nbsp;
quickly understand why the Sand People,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:10.720
as well as the human settlers of the&nbsp;
frontier town Mos Pelgo, fear it.

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:15.520
Consistent with the mistrust dramatized between&nbsp;
settlers and natives in so many Westerns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:19.520
the human settlers in Mos Pelgo are&nbsp;
reluctant to trust the Sand People&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:24.480
until the Mandalorian brokers a tentative&nbsp;
peace. Naturally, the Mandalorian is fluent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:29.840
in two separate dialects of Tusken - one silent,&nbsp;
one spoken - and so is able to explain that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:35.920
nomads are not only capable and rational, but&nbsp;
also honorable people who keep their word.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:40.640
Working together, the humans and Tuskens defeat&nbsp;
their common enemy. Of course many of the Sand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:45.440
People die in the cause, while virtually&nbsp;
all the humans walk away unscathed. Tusken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:50.560
Raiders may have come a long way in the Star Wars&nbsp;
universe, but evidently they are still expendable.

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:55.840
Not much about Tusken culture is seen or&nbsp;
discussed in the Star Wars movies, but as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.840 --> 00:09:00.560
with just about any other species that's even so&nbsp;
much as glimpsed in the background of a scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.560 --> 00:09:05.280
there's a deep well of information about the&nbsp;
Raiders that Star Wars fans can glean from a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:10.960
variety of sources. Indigenous to Tatooine, the&nbsp;
Tusken Raiders not only view outsiders with deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:16.000
and often violent mistrust, but they regard&nbsp;
natural resources - particularly water - with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:21.360
a spiritual level of respect. It's this component&nbsp;
of the Tusken belief system that led them to raid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:26.800
moisture farms and attack travelers who wandered&nbsp;
too close to the planet's scarce natural wells.

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:31.840
Of course, all any longtime Star Wars fan&nbsp;
wants to know about the Tusken Raiders is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:35.920
what they look like under their masks -&nbsp;
a question that seems unlikely to ever be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:41.120
answered in canonical media. The Tuskens have&nbsp;
an almost religious aversion to removing them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:45.040
only showing their faces during life&nbsp;
milestones such as their wedding night.

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:49.360
The Tusken Raiders speak using&nbsp;
a combination of vocal speech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:54.000
and sign language — and as we see in The&nbsp;
Mandalorian, our hero is well-versed in both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:58.880
forms. What casual viewers may not realize,&nbsp;
however, is that Tusken sign language is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:03.840
real — and it was developed by actor Troy&nbsp;
Kotsur, a Mandalorian actor who is deaf&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:08.000
and developed the language to look as little&nbsp;
like American Sign Language as possible.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:11.200
Mandalorian viewers caught a good&nbsp;
glimpse of Kotsur's work during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:16.000
season 2 episode "The Marshal," in which&nbsp;
Mando and Cobb Vanth team up with a Tusken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:20.800
tribe to kill a Krayt Dragon that's been&nbsp;
plaguing the Tatooine outpost of Mos Pelgo.

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:27.760
In late 1977 and early 1978, the first&nbsp;
year after the original film's release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:31.360
Star Wars sold $100 million worth of toys.

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:34.800
"Industry-wide sales are up by almost&nbsp;
eight percent, most of the increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:40.094
attributable to the space toys craze."
"22 million figures. Per year."

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:44.880
Since then, Star Wars toy merchandising&nbsp;
has become a billion-dollar industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:50.560
still thriving today with new action figures,&nbsp;
Funko Pops, and Lego sets introduced regularly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:54.880
There are even books and documentaries devoted&nbsp;
to the creation and selling of the toys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:59.680
as well as many volumes and sites cataloging&nbsp;
them. George Lucas famously negotiated the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:04.320
rights to the Star Wars sequels from 20th&nbsp;
Century Fox partially so he could have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:08.560
merchandising rights as well. One cynical&nbsp;
way of explaining the sheer number of new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:13.840
characters and ships for each new movie is by&nbsp;
considering how many new toys Lucas could peddle.

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:17.520
Along with their more diminutive&nbsp;
fellow Tatooine dwellers the Jawas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:21.760
the "Sand People" as they were then known,&nbsp;
were among the first dozen figures to kick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.760 --> 00:11:26.480
off the global merchandising phenomenon. There&nbsp;
have been many iterations of the figure since,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:31.600
some now going by their proper Tusken Raider&nbsp;
designation. The original vintage action figure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:35.520
now a collector's item, can be had&nbsp;
for hundreds of dollars on Ebay.

